---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TriathlonStats from '../components/TriathlonStats.jsx';
import ReadingGoal from '../components/ReadingGoal.astro';
import Panels from '../components/Panels.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import aboutPic from '../assets/josh-headshot-cropped.jpg';

const pageTitle = "Josh Olivier - About";
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'research-interests', text: 'Research interests' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'hobbies', text: 'Hobbies' },
];
---

<BaseLayout pageTitle={pageTitle} titleHtml="About me" shouldCenterTitle={false} titlePrefix={true} ogImage="/social-cards/about.png">

<Panels headings={headings} />

<Image
    src={aboutPic}
    alt="Josh Olivier and his dog, Oliver"
    style={{
      width: '150px',
      height: 'auto',
      borderRadius: '75px',
    }}
    loading="eager"
/>

      <p>
        I'm a Psychology PhD student in the 
        <a href="https://dzlab.ucr.edu" 
           target="_blank" 
           rel="noopener noreferrer">Dimsdale-Zucker Memory and Context Lab</a>
        at the University of California, Riverside. Before starting my PhD, I spent a few years working as 
        lab manager of the <a href="https://fnim.utdallas.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Functional Neuroimaging of Memory Lab</a> 
        at the University of Texas at Dallas. I study how the brain supports episodic memory and how 
        memory-related brain mechanisms are disrupted by aging and neurological conditions like Alzheimer's disease.
    </p>
        
   <h2 id="research-interests">Research interests</h2>

     <p>  
        I'm interested in understanding episodic memory, which is the ability to recollect previously experienced events (and which 
        <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adn2158" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Endel Tulving</a> 
        famously described as "mental time travel"). Specifically, I'm interested in using neuroimaging tools like 
        <a href="https://fmri.ucsd.edu/Research/whatisfmri.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">fMRI</a> 
        to investigate how the brain encodes information—or forms memories—in everyday life, 
        how it later retrieves this information, and how mechanisms of encoding and retrieval are affected 
        by aging or disrupted by neurological conditions like Alzheimer's disease.
      </p>

      <p>
        We are in the midst of a rapid global demographic shift, with adults over age 60 projected to comprise 
        <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ageing-and-health" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nearly a quarter of the world population by 2050</a>.
        Consequently, the prevalence of age-related cognitive decline and neurodegenerative conditions such as Alzheimer's disease is 
        expected to dramatically increase, taking a massive toll on society at the individual, familial, community, and economic levels. 
        According to a recent 
        <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/why-we-need-to-invest-in-alzheimers-research-now1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Scientific American article</a>, 
        the global economic burden of Alzheimer's disease is expected to reach between $10 and $13.5 trillion by 2050, compared to just $2 trillion in 2019.

        There has never been a more urgent time to invest in scientific research aimed at understanding 
        mechanisms and indicators of age-related cognitive decline. 
      </p>

    <h2 id="hobbies">Hobbies</h2>

    <p>
      Apart from research work, I have many hobbies, including traveling, trying new foods, and learning new skills. 
      I learned Portuguese after spending some time living in 
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazonas_(Brazilian_state)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Amazonas, Brazil</a>, 
      where I enjoyed eating lots of 
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambaqui" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tambaqui</a>,
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tacac%C3%A1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tacacá</a>, 
      and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A%C3%A7a%C3%AD" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">açaí</a>—some of the best food on Earth. 
      I recently visited <a href="/posts/trip-to-lençóis-maranhenses-national-park/">Lençóis Maranhenses National Park</a> in Brazil, which is
      one of the most beautiful places I've been.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some of my other hobbies include <a href="/blog/?tag=3Dprinting">tinkering with my 3D printer</a> and doing triathlons.
      Throughout my triathlon training in <code>{`${new Date().getFullYear()}`}</code>, I've logged the following distances swimming, cycling, 
      and running:
    </p>

<TriathlonStats client:only="preact" />

<p>
  I also enjoy reading, and I'm trying to read more consistently. Check out my <a href="/bookshelf">bookshelf</a> to see a list of books I've read recently, along with 
ratings I've given each book, any reviews I've written, and some fun (and slightly obsessive) <a href="/bookshelf#reading-stats">reading stats</a>. Here's the total number of books I've read in 
  <code>{new Date().getFullYear()}</code>, and my reading goal for the year:
</p>

<ReadingGoal />

</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Delegated glow — catches both server-rendered and client-component stat cards
  document.addEventListener('mousemove', e => {
    const me = e as MouseEvent;
    const card = (me.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLElement>('.stat-card');
    if (!card) return;
    const rect = card.getBoundingClientRect();
    card.style.setProperty('--mouse-x', `${me.clientX - rect.left}px`);
    card.style.setProperty('--mouse-y', `${me.clientY - rect.top}px`);
  });
</script>
